The experiment was performed to observe process state transitions in Linux, including Ready, Running, Waiting, and Terminated states, using monitoring tools such as ps, top, and /proc.
A process was created using the sleep 30 & command, and its process ID and current state were checked using the ps command.
he top command was used to continuously monitor the process, while /proc/PID/status was used to view detailed information about the process.
During execution, the process moves to the Ready state while waiting for CPU allocation, then enters the Running state when selected by the CPU scheduler.
When the process waits for an event or resource, it enters the Waiting state, and after completing its execution, it reaches the Terminated state.
Thus, the experiment successfully demonstrated how Linux processes transition between different states and how these states can be monitored using ps, top, and /proc.
